// ADTs: sugar over renno's native List, not a new Value kind. `Some(5)` IS
// `["Some", 5]`; `None` IS `["None"]` -- see parser::build_ctor_value. That
// means match "just works" against them with the same List patterns
// pattern_matching.rn already uses, and constructors are curried like any
// other multi-arg callable (fold, map): `Cons(1)(rest)`, not `Cons(1, rest)`.
//
// Each constructor also gets a real NOMINAL type, Type::Data("List") --
// e.g. Cons: (Int -> List -> List) -- so a recursive type (List
// referencing itself in Cons's own field list) needs no forward
// declaration: `List` there is just a name compared for equality, nothing
// to resolve. Two `data` types with identically-shaped constructors (both
// wrapping a single Int, say) still aren't interchangeable -- Data is
// name-based, not structural.
//
// `data` also records this type's constructor set for exhaustiveness: the
// match below needs no wildcard arm because { Nil, Cons } is exactly what
// this declaration named -- delete either arm and typecheck rejects the
// program as non-exhaustive.
data List = Nil | Cons(Int, List) in

let rec sum = fun l ->
  match l with
  | Nil -> 0
  | Cons(h, t) -> h + sum(t)
in

sum(Cons(1)(Cons(2)(Cons(3)(Nil))))
